The growing backlash against the proposed Stratos AI data center in Box Elder County ought to be setting off alarm bells for Utah leaders.

This project appears to have been pushed forward awfully fast with very little public input for something that could dramatically affect Utah’s water supply, power grid, environment, and economy for decades to come.

And now people all across the state are paying attention.

Utahns are asking some pretty fair questions about the enormous energy and water demands tied to this hyperscale AI project, especially with the Great Salt Lake already struggling and rural infrastructure stretched thin in many areas. Others are questioning the political and corporate connections surrounding the project and wondering why so much of this seems to be happening behind closed doors.

And frankly, I think those concerns are justified.

The opposition movement is growing quickly. Community members, researchers, environmental advocates, local organizers, and ordinary citizens are coordinating rallies, town halls, investigations, media outreach, and public pressure campaigns. Coverage has now spread from local Utah media to national and even international outlets.

What started as a local issue is turning into a much larger conversation about transparency, public resources, and whether regular Utahns still have a meaningful voice when billion-dollar projects come knocking.

To be clear, this is not about being anti-technology. Utah should absolutely be part of the future economy. We want good jobs, innovation, and economic opportunity.

But there’s a big difference between responsible growth and handing over massive amounts of land, water, energy, and political influence to billionaire-backed corporations without fully hearing out the people who actually live here.

A lot of rural Utahns already feel like decisions get made in Salt Lake boardrooms and political backchannels long before the public ever gets a chance to weigh in. This project is starting to feel like another example of exactly that.

People are tired of being told to just trust the process while the details keep changing and the stakes keep getting bigger.

And regardless of where you fall politically, I’d hope we can agree on a few basic principles:
The public deserves transparency.
Local communities deserve a real voice.
Environmental impacts deserve honest scrutiny.
And Utah’s water and energy future should belong to the people of Utah, not just whichever corporations happen to have the deepest pockets.
At the end of the day, people simply want honesty, accountability, and an actual seat at the table before projects this massive move forward. That doesn’t sound radical to me. It sounds like common sense.
Also, if a project reportedly needs more electricity than common sense… maybe we ought to slow down for five minutes and ask a few extra questions.
